-- A member may be referred by another member, never by itself, and may be suspended. The
-- prohibition is written as a named refinement and `never SelfReferral`. `velle describe` on
-- this file prints, among its lines:
--
--   - Its suspended is a boolean, starting as false.
--   - A Member is an ActiveMember when its suspended is false.
--   ...
--   ## What is never allowed
--
--   ### Member
--
--   - A SelfReferral is forbidden when it is a SelfReferral.
--     In the spec: 44-describe-tautology-and-boolean-accepted.velle, line 27, `never SelfReferral`
--
-- "A SelfReferral is forbidden when it is a SelfReferral" is true and says nothing: a reader
-- would expect "A Member is forbidden when it is a SelfReferral", or the refinement's own words,
-- as the same prohibition written in place draws: "A Member is forbidden when its referredBy is
-- present and its referredBy is it." for the last line of this file. "Its suspended is a boolean" and "its suspended is false" name a boolean field as a
-- noun; a reader would expect "It may be suspended, starting as not suspended" and "when it is
-- not suspended".
shape Member {
    name: text
    referredBy: one Member?
    suspended: boolean initially false
}
shape SelfReferral = Member where referredBy is some and referredBy == this
never SelfReferral
shape ActiveMember = Member where not suspended
never (Member where referredBy is some and referredBy == this)
